{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","title":"Snell's law of refraction"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Refraction is a change in direction when light crosses between materials. Measure angles from the dashed normal, not the surface.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"refraction","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.08,0.5],[0.92,0.5]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"boundary between air and glass"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.1],[0.5,0.91]],"stroke":"dashed","label":"normal"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.8,0.25],"content":"air"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.8,0.76],"content":"glass"}]},{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Snell's law"},{"op":"equation","id":"indices","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"n_1=1.00,\\qquad n_2=1.50"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The incoming ray makes a forty-five-degree angle with the normal in air. This is the angle of incidence.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"incident","canvas_id":"refraction","from":[0.18,0.18],"to":[0.5,0.5],"stroke":"solid"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"incangle","canvas_id":"refraction","center":[0.5,0.5],"radius":0.14,"start_deg":225,"end_deg":270,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ₁"},{"op":"equation","id":"givenangle","anchor":{"el":"indices","side":"below","gap":0.07},"latex":"\\theta_1=45^\\circ"},{"op":"equation","id":"snell","anchor":{"el":"givenangle","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"n_1\\sin\\theta_1=n_2\\sin\\theta_2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Glass has the larger refractive index, so the transmitted ray bends toward the normal. Its angle must be smaller than forty-five degrees.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"refracted","canvas_id":"refraction","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.7,0.88],"stroke":"solid"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"refrangle","canvas_id":"refraction","center":[0.5,0.5],"radius":0.17,"start_deg":62,"end_deg":90,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ₂"},{"op":"equation","id":"substitute","anchor":{"el":"snell","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\sin\\theta_2=\\frac{1.00}{1.50}\\sin45^\\circ\\approx0.471"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why is the refracted angle smaller than the incident angle here?","expected_gist":"Light enters a higher-index material, so it bends toward the normal."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Taking inverse sine gives about twenty-eight degrees. The smaller angle confirms bending toward the normal; both angles are measured from that normal.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"substitute","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\theta_2=\\sin^{-1}(0.471)\\approx28.1^\\circ"},{"op":"point","id":"exitpoint","canvas_id":"refraction","at":[0.7,0.88],"label":"28.1° to normal"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"a63cdb24-2a5e-4709-a815-8374a9af1710","accepted_steps":4,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
